var e=[{id:`gate-driver`,name:`Half-bridge gate driver IC`,category:`control`,symbol:`gate-driver`,plainSummary:`The controller chip speaks in tiny 3.3 V signals, but the power switches need a strong, fast 12 V kick to turn fully on and off. The gate driver is the muscle in between.`,function:`Converts logic-level PWM signals into high-current gate pulses for one high-side and one low-side MOSFET in each bridge leg.`,operatingPrinciple:`Totem-pole output stages source and sink several amps to charge the switch gate capacitance in tens of nanoseconds. The high-side output floats on the switching node (HS). Its supply comes from a bootstrap capacitor between HB and HS, which charges through a diode from VDD whenever the low-side switch is on. Under-voltage lockout holds outputs low if VDD is too low to drive the gates fully.`,applications:[`H-bridge and half-bridge inverters`,`Synchronous buck/boost converters`,`Motor drives`],terminals:[{id:`p1`,label:`1 VDD`,role:`power-in`,description:`Driver supply, 8–17 V (12 V here).`},{id:`p2`,label:`2 HB`,role:`power-in`,description:`High-side bootstrap supply (bootstrap capacitor to HS).`},{id:`p3`,label:`3 HO`,role:`control`,description:`High-side gate output, to the high-side MOSFET gate via the gate resistor.`},{id:`p4`,label:`4 HS`,role:`sense`,description:`High-side source return, connected to the bridge switching node.`},{id:`p5`,label:`5 HI`,role:`control`,description:`High-side logic input from the controller.`},{id:`p6`,label:`6 LI`,role:`control`,description:`Low-side logic input from the controller.`},{id:`p7`,label:`7 VSS`,role:`ground`,description:`Ground, connected to the low-side MOSFET source.`},{id:`p8`,label:`8 LO`,role:`control`,description:`Low-side gate output.`}],ioBehaviour:`Inputs: 3.3/5 V logic PWM. Outputs: 0–12 V gate pulses with up to about 4 A peak; propagation delay ≈ 20 ns.`,failureSymptoms:[`No gate pulses: bridge leg dead, no output`,`Driver cracked or burnt after a MOSFET failure (gate punch-through)`,`Missing high-side pulses: failed bootstrap diode or capacitor`],replacementNotes:[`The pinout shown is a representative 8-pin half-bridge driver arrangement used in this course; pinouts differ between driver families, so always use the exact part number and its datasheet.`,`Check the bootstrap diode, capacitor and gate resistors at the same time.`],safety:[`HO and HS swing at bus potential; never ground-reference a scope probe on HS.`,`Static-sensitive.`],related:[`mosfet`,`microcontroller`,`pwm-controller`,`diode`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Driver supply VDD`,value:`12`,unit:`V`,kind:`model`},{name:`Peak output current of this driver class`,value:`≈ 2–4`,unit:`A`,kind:`typical`},{name:`High-side bootstrap voltage class for 48 V bridges`,value:`≈ 100–120`,unit:`V`,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Gate resistor sets dV/dt: smaller R gives faster edges and lower switching loss, but more EMI and overshoot. Size the bootstrap capacitor at ≥ 10× the total gate charge divided by allowed ripple (C_boot ≥ 10·Q_g/ΔV). At 100 % duty the bootstrap cannot recharge, which limits maximum modulation.`},{id:`pwm-controller`,name:`PWM controller IC (SG3525 type)`,category:`control`,symbol:`pwm-controller`,plainSummary:`A dedicated chip that produces the on/off timing pulses. It sets how fast the switches flip and for how long each one stays on. Many simpler inverters use it as their only "brain".`,function:`Generates the PWM drive for the MPPT charger stage and provides a hardware shutdown path independent of the microcontroller.`,operatingPrinciple:`An oscillator charges a timing capacitor C_T through a current set by R_T, producing a sawtooth ramp at f_osc ≈ 1/(C_T·(0.7·R_T + 3·R_D)). A comparator compares the ramp with an error-amplifier output to set duty cycle. A flip-flop steers alternate pulses to outputs A and B, giving push-pull drive with dead-time set by R_D. Soft-start and shutdown pins provide protection.`,applications:[`Push-pull and half-bridge converters`,`Square-wave and modified-sine inverters`,`Charger stages`],terminals:[{id:`p1`,label:`1 INV IN`,role:`sense`,description:`Error amplifier inverting input (feedback).`},{id:`p2`,label:`2 NI IN`,role:`sense`,description:`Error amplifier non-inverting input (reference).`},{id:`p3`,label:`3 SYNC`,role:`control`,description:`Oscillator synchronisation input.`},{id:`p4`,label:`4 OSC OUT`,role:`control`,description:`Oscillator output.`},{id:`p5`,label:`5 CT`,role:`passive`,description:`Timing capacitor.`},{id:`p6`,label:`6 RT`,role:`passive`,description:`Timing resistor.`},{id:`p7`,label:`7 DISCHARGE`,role:`passive`,description:`Dead-time resistor between CT and this pin.`},{id:`p8`,label:`8 SOFT START`,role:`passive`,description:`Soft-start capacitor.`},{id:`p9`,label:`9 COMP`,role:`passive`,description:`Error amplifier output / compensation.`},{id:`p10`,label:`10 SHUTDOWN`,role:`control`,description:`High = outputs off (protection input).`},{id:`p11`,label:`11 OUT A`,role:`control`,description:`Output A.`},{id:`p12`,label:`12 GND`,role:`ground`,description:`Ground.`},{id:`p13`,label:`13 VC`,role:`power-in`,description:`Output stage collector supply.`},{id:`p14`,label:`14 OUT B`,role:`control`,description:`Output B.`},{id:`p15`,label:`15 VIN`,role:`power-in`,description:`IC supply, 8–35 V.`},{id:`p16`,label:`16 VREF`,role:`power-out`,description:`5.1 V reference output.`}],ioBehaviour:`Inputs: feedback voltage, shutdown logic. Outputs: two alternating PWM signals (up to about 400 kHz) with adjustable dead-time.`,failureSymptoms:[`No output pulses; VREF (pin 16) missing`,`Frequency drift (failed timing capacitor)`,`Stuck in shutdown because pin 10 is held high by a protection fault`],replacementNotes:[`The SG3525 is an industry-standard part type made by several manufacturers: use an SG3525-type part in the same package and check the suffix grade on the datasheet; DIP parts are often socketed.`,`Note the orientation notch / pin-1 dot.`],safety:[`Low-voltage control circuit, but on a board that also carries bus voltages.`],related:[`gate-driver`,`microcontroller`,`mosfet`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`SG3525 reference voltage`,value:`5.1`,unit:`V`,kind:`part-type`},{name:`SG3525 oscillator range`,value:`100 Hz – 400 kHz`,unit:``,kind:`part-type`},{name:`SG3525 supply range`,value:`8–35`,unit:`V`,kind:`part-type`}],advancedNote:`Push-pull outputs limit each output to < 50 % duty, which inherently prevents both switches in a leg conducting together. Design dead-time to exceed the switch turn-off delay plus fall time. Compensate the error amplifier (pin 9) for the converter's LC double pole.`},{id:`microcontroller`,name:`Microcontroller (MCU)`,category:`control`,symbol:`microcontroller`,plainSummary:`The inverter's brain: a small computer on one chip. It measures voltages and currents, decides what each switch should do, creates the sine-wave pattern, protects the system and talks to the display.`,function:`Generates SPWM for the H-bridge, runs voltage and current control loops, MPPT tracking, battery charging, protection and the display interface.`,operatingPrinciple:`Firmware samples the voltage and current sensors with the on-chip ADC tens of thousands of times per second, computes the duty cycle needed to follow a 50 Hz sine reference (closed-loop control), and loads it into high-resolution PWM timers. Protection routines compare readings with limits and disable the PWM within microseconds if, for example, current exceeds the overload threshold.`,applications:[`Digital power-converter control`,`MPPT algorithms`,`Battery management`,`Communications and data logging`],terminals:[{id:`vdd`,label:`VDD / VDDA`,role:`power-in`,description:`3.3 V supply pins (digital and analogue), with local decoupling.`},{id:`vss`,label:`VSS / VSSA`,role:`ground`,description:`Ground pins.`},{id:`nrst`,label:`NRST`,role:`control`,description:`Active-low reset.`},{id:`boot0`,label:`BOOT0`,role:`control`,description:`Selects boot from flash (low) or bootloader (high).`},{id:`pwm`,label:`PWM outputs`,role:`control`,description:`High-resolution timer outputs to the gate drivers.`},{id:`adc`,label:`ADC inputs`,role:`sense`,description:`Analogue inputs from voltage and current sensors.`},{id:`swd`,label:`SWDIO / SWCLK`,role:`control`,description:`Programming and debug interface.`}],ioBehaviour:`Inputs: 0–3.3 V analogue sensor signals, button and communication inputs. Outputs: 3.3 V PWM signals, relay and fan control, display data.`,failureSymptoms:[`Display blank, no response (no 3.3 V or no clock)`,`Random resets under load (supply noise, decoupling)`,`Corrupted firmware: stuck on boot screen`,`Wrong readings (ADC reference fault)`],replacementNotes:[`Requires the exact part and the manufacturer's firmware; usually the whole control board is replaced. Pin functions here are shown as groups because pin numbers differ between MCU families.`,`Fine-pitch package (0.5 mm): hot-air rework skills required.`],safety:[`Control ground may be referenced to the battery negative; use isolated programmers and probes.`],related:[`gate-driver`,`pwm-controller`,`voltage-sensor`,`current-sensor`,`display-board`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Core clock of power-conversion MCUs`,value:`tens to hundreds of`,unit:`MHz`,kind:`typical`},{name:`ADC resolution`,value:`12`,unit:`bit (typical)`,kind:`typical`},{name:`Logic supply`,value:`3.3`,unit:`V`,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Control-loop bandwidth must sit well below the PWM frequency but far above 50 Hz to reject load disturbances; sample the current at the PWM centre to avoid switching noise. Hardware comparators wired to the timer break input give cycle-by-cycle protection that does not depend on firmware timing.`},{id:`display-board`,name:`Display and user-interface board`,category:`control`,symbol:`display-board`,plainSummary:`The front screen and buttons. It shows battery level, solar power, load, and fault codes, and lets the user change settings.`,function:`Shows operating data (input/output voltage, power, battery state of charge, mode) and fault codes; accepts settings via buttons.`,operatingPrinciple:`An LCD module and status LEDs are driven by the microcontroller over a serial bus (I²C or UART). Buttons are read as digital inputs. The board is powered from the control 5 V/3.3 V supply.`,applications:[`Inverter and UPS front panels`,`Charge controller displays`],terminals:[{id:`vcc`,label:`VCC`,role:`power-in`,description:`5 V or 3.3 V supply from the control board.`},{id:`gnd`,label:`GND`,role:`ground`,description:`Ground.`},{id:`sda`,label:`SDA / TX`,role:`control`,description:`Serial data.`},{id:`scl`,label:`SCL / RX`,role:`control`,description:`Serial clock / receive.`}],ioBehaviour:`Input: serial data frames from the MCU and button presses. Output: visual information to the user and button states to the MCU.`,failureSymptoms:[`Blank screen but inverter works (ribbon cable, backlight, supply)`,`Garbled characters (communication fault)`,`Buttons unresponsive (contamination, broken switch)`],replacementNotes:[`Must match the firmware protocol; use the manufacturer's board.`,`Handle the ribbon connector latch carefully.`],safety:[`Normally low voltage; ensure it is not referenced to a live bus before probing.`],related:[`microcontroller`,`main-pcb`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Supply`,value:`5`,unit:`V`,kind:`model`},{name:`Interface`,value:`I²C or UART serial`,unit:``,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Keep the display interface isolated or referenced to protective earth when the control ground floats at battery potential. Fault-code tables are the fastest diagnostic route in the repair workshop.`},{id:`main-pcb`,name:`Main printed circuit board (power board)`,category:`control`,symbol:`main-pcb`,plainSummary:`The board that holds and connects the parts. Copper tracks act as wires, and wide, thick tracks carry the large currents.`,function:`Mechanically supports and electrically interconnects the power stage, sensors, auxiliary supply and connectors.`,operatingPrinciple:`Copper traces on an FR-4 glass-epoxy laminate form the circuit. Trace width and copper thickness (e.g. 70 µm = 2 oz) set current capacity. Creepage and clearance distances separate high-voltage and low-voltage areas. Solder mask (green) prevents bridging, and the silkscreen prints reference designators (Q1, C3, …) that link the board to the schematic.`,applications:[`Every inverter, charger and converter`],terminals:[{id:`j-dc`,label:`DC input pads`,role:`power-in`,description:`Bolted lugs where the battery DC enters the board.`},{id:`j-ac`,label:`AC output pads`,role:`power-out`,description:`Connection to the transformer and output relay.`},{id:`j-ctrl`,label:`Control connector`,role:`control`,description:`Ribbon connector to the control board.`}],ioBehaviour:`Passive interconnect; its resistance and inductance affect losses and switching overshoot.`,failureSymptoms:[`Burnt or lifted traces near power parts`,`Cracked solder joints on heavy components (thermal cycling)`,`Carbon tracking between high-voltage pads (dust, moisture)`],replacementNotes:[`Repair small trace damage with wire of equal cross-section; replace boards with carbonised areas.`,`Clean with isopropyl alcohol; conformal coat if originally coated.`],safety:[`Different regions sit at battery, PV and mains potential; know which area you are touching.`],related:[`mosfet`,`capacitor`,`gate-driver`,`dc-input`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Copper weight for power boards`,value:`2`,unit:`oz (70 µm)`,kind:`typical`},{name:`Board thickness (FR-4)`,value:`1.6`,unit:`mm`,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Minimise the area of the high-di/dt commutation loop (bus capacitor → high-side switch → low-side switch) to reduce overshoot and EMI; place ceramic capacitors directly across each leg. Use separate power and signal grounds joined at one point.`}],t=[{id:`battery-terminals`,name:`Battery terminals`,category:`battery-charging`,symbol:`battery-terminals`,plainSummary:`Heavy bolt posts where the thick battery cables connect. Red is positive (+) and black is negative (−). Connecting them the wrong way round can destroy the inverter instantly.`,function:`Connect the 48 V battery bank to the inverter with low resistance for currents up to about 80 A.`,operatingPrinciple:`A threaded stud clamps a crimped ring lug against a contact plate. Contact resistance must stay in the micro-ohm range: at 70 A, even 1 mΩ dissipates I²R = 4.9 W, so loose or corroded joints overheat.`,applications:[`Inverter and charger battery inputs`,`Battery bank interconnections`],terminals:[{id:`+`,label:`+ (red)`,role:`power-in`,description:`Battery positive, via the battery fuse.`},{id:`-`,label:`− (black)`,role:`ground`,description:`Battery negative.`}],ioBehaviour:`DC input: 40–60 V for a 48 V nominal bank, currents to ~ 80 A discharging, ~ 60 A charging.`,failureSymptoms:[`Melted insulation or discoloured lugs (loose connection)`,`Low-battery shutdowns under load despite charged battery (voltage drop at terminals)`,`Inverter destroyed after reverse connection`],replacementNotes:[`Use correctly sized crimped lugs and cable (e.g. 25–35 mm² for 80 A).`,`Torque to specification and fit insulating boots.`],safety:[`A battery short circuit can deliver thousands of amps: remove jewellery, use insulated tools, connect positive first and disconnect negative first (or as the manufacturer specifies).`,`Reverse polarity can cause fire.`],related:[`fuse`,`dc-input`,`capacitor`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Stud size`,value:`M8`,unit:``,kind:`model`},{name:`Maximum continuous current`,value:`100`,unit:`A`,kind:`model`},{name:`Cable size for ≈ 80 A`,value:`25–35`,unit:`mm²`,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Connecting a battery to a discharged capacitor bank produces a large inrush spark; good designs include a pre-charge resistor or switch. Reverse-polarity protection by MOSFET or fuse-and-diode costs efficiency, so many inverters rely on the fuse.`},{id:`dc-input`,name:`DC input terminals (power board)`,category:`io-connection`,symbol:`dc-input`,plainSummary:`Where the battery power enters the main board, after passing the fuse. Two heavy screw lugs marked DC+ and DC−.`,function:`Bolt-down lugs on the power board that bring battery DC (after the fuse) to the DC bus capacitors and H-bridge.`,operatingPrinciple:`PCB-mounted threaded lugs connect cable lugs to heavy copper planes. The low inductance from these lugs to the bus capacitors matters for switching performance.`,applications:[`Power board DC entry points`],terminals:[{id:`dc+`,label:`DC+`,role:`power-in`,description:`From battery positive via the fuse.`},{id:`dc-`,label:`DC−`,role:`ground`,description:`From battery negative.`}],ioBehaviour:`Input: battery voltage (40–60 V) after the fuse. Output: DC to the bus capacitors and H-bridge. Carries the full battery current plus 100 Hz ripple current drawn by the single-phase output stage.`,failureSymptoms:[`Overheated lug and charred PCB around it`,`Intermittent shutdown when cables move`],replacementNotes:[`Lug replacement requires heavy soldering; inspect copper planes for delamination.`],safety:[`Live at battery voltage whenever the battery and fuse are connected.`],related:[`battery-terminals`,`fuse`,`main-pcb`,`capacitor`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Rated current`,value:`100`,unit:`A`,kind:`model`},{name:`Nominal voltage`,value:`48 (40–60 operating)`,unit:`V`,kind:`model`}],advancedNote:`Laminated busbars or overlapping planes minimise the loop inductance between the DC input, bus capacitors and switches.`},{id:`ac-output`,name:`AC output terminal block`,category:`io-connection`,symbol:`ac-output`,plainSummary:`Screw terminals where the 230 V AC output connects to the house wiring or loads: L (live, brown), N (neutral, blue) and PE (earth, green/yellow).`,function:`Connects the inverter output to the AC distribution board with correct identification of line, neutral and protective earth.`,operatingPrinciple:`Rising-clamp screw terminals grip the conductors. IEC colour coding: brown = line (L), blue = neutral (N), green/yellow = protective earth (PE).`,applications:[`Inverter outputs`,`Grid input terminals of hybrid inverters`],terminals:[{id:`L`,label:`L (brown)`,role:`power-out`,description:`Line: 230 V relative to N and PE.`},{id:`N`,label:`N (blue)`,role:`power-out`,description:`Neutral: return conductor.`},{id:`PE`,label:`PE (green/yellow)`,role:`earth`,description:`Protective earth, bonded to the enclosure.`}],ioBehaviour:`Output: 230 V rms ± 5 %, 50 Hz, up to 13 A continuous (3 kW).`,failureSymptoms:[`Burnt terminal from loose screw`,`Swapped L and N (dangerous for some appliances)`,`Missing PE connection (enclosure could become live in a fault)`],replacementNotes:[`Use correctly rated terminals; torque screws; ferrules on stranded wire.`],safety:[`Mains voltage is lethal. Only qualified persons work on live AC terminals.`,`An inverter can back-feed its output from the battery even when the grid is off.`],related:[`relay`,`transformer`,`protective-earth`,`circuit-breaker`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Output voltage`,value:`230 ± 5 %`,unit:`V~`,kind:`model`},{name:`Continuous output current at 3 kW`,value:`≈ 13`,unit:`A`,kind:`model`},{name:`Colour code (IEC)`,value:`brown L · blue N · green/yellow PE`,unit:``,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Neutral-earth bonding differs between off-grid and grid-connected operation: an off-grid inverter usually needs its own N-PE bond so RCDs work, while in bypass mode the grid's bond must be used. Transfer relays therefore often switch the neutral too.`},{id:`solar-input`,name:`Solar (PV) input connectors (MC4 type)`,category:`io-connection`,symbol:`solar-input`,plainSummary:`Weatherproof click-together connectors for the solar panel cables, marked PV+ and PV−. Panels make voltage whenever light falls on them, so these can be live even when everything is "off".`,function:`Connect the PV string to the inverter's MPPT charger through the DC breaker.`,operatingPrinciple:`MC4-type connectors use a male pin and female socket with locking latches and seals (IP67). Male and female housings are paired so positive and negative cannot be cross-connected between matching equipment.`,applications:[`PV array strings`,`Hybrid and grid-tied inverter PV inputs`],terminals:[{id:`pv+`,label:`PV+`,role:`power-in`,description:`String positive. On most equipment, check the moulded "+" mark.`},{id:`pv-`,label:`PV−`,role:`power-in`,description:`String negative, the opposite-gender connector.`}],ioBehaviour:`DC input: up to 450 V open-circuit, about 12 A short-circuit per string.`,failureSymptoms:[`No solar charging; PV voltage reads 0 at the inverter`,`Melted connector (mismatched brands, poor crimp)`,`Earth-fault warnings (damaged cable insulation)`],replacementNotes:[`Only mate connectors of the same manufacturer and type; use the correct crimp tool.`],safety:[`PV voltage is present in daylight; cover panels or work at night if connectors must be handled.`,`Never disconnect under load; open the DC breaker first to avoid an arc.`],related:[`circuit-breaker`,`igbt`,`capacitor`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Inverter PV input maximum`,value:`450`,unit:`V`,kind:`model`},{name:`MC4-type connector voltage classes`,value:`1000 or 1500`,unit:`V DC`,kind:`typical`},{name:`MC4-type connector current classes`,value:`≈ 20–40`,unit:`A`,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Size strings so the cold-weather V_oc stays below the input maximum and the hot-weather V_mp stays above the MPPT lower limit. MPPT efficiency depends on tracking speed under passing clouds.`},{id:`protective-earth`,name:`Protective earth (PE) stud`,category:`protection`,symbol:`protective-earth`,plainSummary:`A bolt that connects the metal case to earth with a green/yellow wire. If a live wire ever touches the case, current flows to earth and trips the protection instead of through a person.`,function:`Bonds the enclosure, heat sink, transformer core and PE terminal together and to the installation earth.`,operatingPrinciple:`A low-impedance path to earth ensures that an insulation fault to the metal case causes a large fault current. That current operates the fuse, breaker or RCD quickly and keeps the touch voltage low. The stud is marked with the earth symbol (IEC 60417-5019).`,applications:[`Every Class I (earthed) electrical appliance`],terminals:[{id:`pe`,label:`PE ⏚`,role:`earth`,description:`Earth stud with star washer and ring lug.`}],ioBehaviour:`Carries no current in normal operation; carries fault current briefly during an insulation failure.`,failureSymptoms:[`High earth-continuity resistance (loose or painted contact)`,`Tingling from the case (missing earth plus leakage)`,`RCD does not trip in a fault`],replacementNotes:[`Never omit the earth bond after maintenance; reconnect every bonding wire that was removed.`],safety:[`Disconnecting PE removes protection for everyone who touches the case.`],related:[`ac-output`,`heat-sink`,`transformer`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Earth-continuity test limit`,value:`≤ 0.1 Ω plus test-lead resistance`,unit:``,kind:`typical`},{name:`Bonding conductor in this model`,value:`6`,unit:`mm²`,kind:`model`}],advancedNote:`Protective bonding is verified with a high-current (e.g. 10–25 A) continuity test in production; a multimeter test only proves the path exists. The earth stud must not double as a component mounting screw, so that it is never removed accidentally.`}],n=[{id:`mosfet`,name:`Power MOSFET (N-channel)`,category:`power-switching`,symbol:`mosfet`,plainSummary:`A very fast electronic switch with no moving parts. A small voltage on the gate turns a large battery current on or off thousands of times a second. Four of them, arranged as an H-bridge, make the current flow first one way and then the other through the transformer, which is how DC becomes AC.`,function:`Switches the battery-side current in the H-bridge. In this inverter four MOSFETs alternately connect each end of the transformer primary to the 48 V bus or to ground.`,operatingPrinciple:`A voltage between gate and source (V_GS) above the threshold (typically 2–4 V) creates a conductive channel between drain and source. Fully enhanced at V_GS ≈ 10–12 V, the channel behaves like a small resistor R_DS(on), so conduction loss is I²·R_DS(on). The gate is insulated, so it draws current only while its capacitance charges or discharges during a switching edge. An intrinsic body diode from source to drain conducts reverse current.`,applications:[`Low-voltage H-bridges in 12/24/48 V inverters`,`DC-DC converters and MPPT chargers`,`Battery protection switches in BMS units`,`Motor drives`],terminals:[{id:`g`,label:`G (pin 1)`,role:`control`,description:`Gate. Left-hand lead when the marked face is towards you and the leads point down.`},{id:`d`,label:`D (pin 2)`,role:`power-in`,description:`Drain. Centre lead; internally connected to the metal tab.`},{id:`s`,label:`S (pin 3)`,role:`power-out`,description:`Source. Right-hand lead; return path for both load current and gate drive.`},{id:`tab`,label:`Tab = D`,role:`power-in`,description:`Metal mounting tab, electrically the drain. Must be insulated from an earthed heat sink with a thermal pad and bush.`}],ioBehaviour:`Input: gate-source voltage from the gate driver (0 V = off, 10–12 V = on). Output: drain-source path that is either open (blocks up to V_DSS) or a few milliohms. Switching edges take tens to hundreds of nanoseconds.`,failureSymptoms:[`Drain-source short circuit: fuse blows or overcurrent protection trips immediately at power-on`,`Gate-source short (often after a voltage spike): gate driver overheats, no switching`,`Open circuit: one half of the output waveform missing, transformer hum changes, output low`,`Overheating or a discoloured board area around the tab`],replacementNotes:[`Match or exceed V_DSS, continuous I_D, and match R_DS(on) and gate charge Q_g closely, so the gate driver and heating stay the same.`,`Replace all MOSFETs in a failed leg (often the whole bridge); a surviving device may be weakened.`,`Fit a new insulating thermal pad and bush; torque the clamp screw to the datasheet value.`,`Check the gate resistor and gate driver before powering up; a failed driver will destroy new MOSFETs.`],safety:[`The tab is live at drain potential during operation.`,`Static-sensitive: handle by the body with an earthed wrist strap.`,`Never power the bridge with a MOSFET missing from a leg that is still driven.`],related:[`gate-driver`,`heat-sink`,`transformer`,`capacitor`,`igbt`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`V_DSS needed for a 48 V bridge`,value:`100–150`,unit:`V`,kind:`typical`},{name:`R_DS(on) of low-voltage power MOSFETs`,value:`≈ 1–10`,unit:`mΩ`,kind:`typical`},{name:`Total gate charge Q_g`,value:`tens to a few hundred`,unit:`nC`,kind:`typical`},{name:`Devices in the LF-3K bridge`,value:`4`,unit:``,kind:`model`}],advancedNote:`A 48 V bus should use parts rated at least 100 V, because leakage inductance produces voltage overshoot at turn-off. Losses split into conduction loss, I_rms²·R_DS(on) rising with temperature (about 1.7× at 125 °C), and switching loss, ½·V·I·(t_r+t_f)·f_sw. Paralleled devices share current well because R_DS(on) rises with temperature, but each needs its own gate resistor to prevent oscillation.`},{id:`igbt`,name:`IGBT (Insulated-Gate Bipolar Transistor)`,category:`power-switching`,symbol:`igbt`,plainSummary:`A power switch for higher voltages. It is controlled like a MOSFET, with a voltage on the gate, but carries current like a bipolar transistor, so it handles hundreds of volts efficiently. Here it switches the high-voltage solar input.`,function:`High-side switch of the MPPT buck charger that converts the solar array voltage (up to 450 V) down to the 48 V battery.`,operatingPrinciple:`A MOSFET structure on the input drives a PNP bipolar output stage. When V_GE exceeds the threshold the device conducts with a nearly constant on-state voltage V_CE(sat), typically 1.5–2 V, instead of a resistance. Conduction loss is therefore ≈ V_CE(sat)·I, which favours IGBTs at high voltage and high current. At turn-off, stored minority carriers cause a "tail current" that limits practical switching frequency to about 20–40 kHz.`,applications:[`High-voltage MPPT chargers and PV boost stages`,`Grid-tied inverter bridges (400–800 V DC bus)`,`Motor drives and welding inverters`,`Induction heating`],terminals:[{id:`g`,label:`G (pin 1)`,role:`control`,description:`Gate. Left lead, marked face towards you.`},{id:`c`,label:`C (pin 2)`,role:`power-in`,description:`Collector. Centre lead, connected to the tab.`},{id:`e`,label:`E (pin 3)`,role:`power-out`,description:`Emitter. Right lead; gate-drive return.`},{id:`tab`,label:`Tab = C`,role:`power-in`,description:`Collector tab, live at up to the PV voltage. Insulate from the heat sink.`}],ioBehaviour:`Input: gate-emitter voltage, typically +15 V on and 0 V or −8 V off. Output: collector-emitter path that blocks up to V_CES or conducts with ≈1.6–2 V drop. Most discrete IGBTs include a co-packaged anti-parallel diode.`,failureSymptoms:[`Collector-emitter short: PV input breaker or fuse trips, charger disabled with a fault code`,`No solar charging while PV voltage is present`,`Excessive heating at low switching frequency (degraded V_CE(sat))`],replacementNotes:[`Match V_CES, current rating, V_CE(sat), switching speed class and whether an anti-parallel diode is included.`,`A faster IGBT can ring or overshoot in the original layout; keep the same gate resistor.`,`Renew the insulating pad and check the snubber components.`],safety:[`Works at PV string voltage (hazardous DC). Solar panels keep producing voltage in daylight; isolate at the PV disconnect.`,`DC arcs do not self-extinguish like AC arcs; never break a PV connection under load.`],related:[`mosfet`,`solar-input`,`gate-driver`,`heat-sink`,`diode`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Common V_CES classes`,value:`600–1200`,unit:`V`,kind:`typical`},{name:`V_CE(sat) at rated current`,value:`≈ 1.5–2.5`,unit:`V`,kind:`typical`},{name:`Practical switching frequency (discrete)`,value:`up to ≈ 20–40`,unit:`kHz`,kind:`typical`},{name:`PV input limit the LF-3K charger must block`,value:`450`,unit:`V`,kind:`model`}],advancedNote:`Choose an IGBT over a MOSFET when V_CE(sat)·I is smaller than I²·R_DS(on) at the operating current and voltage, which is usually above about 400 V. The tail current makes turn-off energy E_off the dominant switching loss, so P_sw ≈ (E_on + E_off)·f_sw from the datasheet curves. Negative turn-off gate voltage and a Miller clamp prevent dv/dt-induced false turn-on.`},{id:`transformer`,name:`Line-frequency transformer`,category:`power-conversion`,symbol:`transformer`,plainSummary:`Two coils of copper wire wound around an iron core. The H-bridge feeds a low voltage into the thick primary coil and the thin secondary coil delivers 230 V. It also isolates the battery side from the mains side.`,function:`Steps the 48 V H-bridge output up to 230 V AC and provides galvanic isolation between the battery and the AC output.`,operatingPrinciple:`An alternating current in the primary produces an alternating magnetic flux in the laminated steel core (Faraday's law). The flux links the secondary and induces a voltage in proportion to the turns ratio: V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p. Power is conserved apart from losses, so the current ratio is inverse. Laminations reduce eddy-current losses. The core must not saturate, which requires V/(f·N) to stay below the design limit, so frequency matters.`,applications:[`Low-frequency (LF) inverters and UPS units`,`Mains isolation transformers`,`Battery chargers that run the inverter bridge in reverse`,`Welding sets`],terminals:[{id:`p1`,label:`P1`,role:`power-in`,description:`Primary winding start (thick conductor), from H-bridge leg A.`},{id:`p2`,label:`P2`,role:`power-in`,description:`Primary winding finish, from H-bridge leg B.`},{id:`s1`,label:`S1`,role:`power-out`,description:`Secondary start, to output filter and line (L) via the output relay.`},{id:`s2`,label:`S2`,role:`power-out`,description:`Secondary finish, to neutral (N).`}],ioBehaviour:`Input: low-voltage AC from the bridge, roughly 30 V rms at full modulation. Output: 230 V rms at 50 Hz. In charge mode, power flows backwards: the mains feeds the secondary and the bridge rectifies the primary to charge the battery.`,failureSymptoms:[`Burnt smell, darkened or blistered winding varnish`,`Very loud hum or buzzing (loose laminations, overload or DC offset saturation)`,`Open winding: no output at all, bridge sees no load current`,`Shorted turns: high no-load current, overheating, low output voltage`],replacementNotes:[`Match VA rating, voltage ratio, frequency, and insulation class.`,`Transformers are heavy (about 20–30 kg for 3 kVA); support the weight and use all mounting bolts.`,`Restore the protective-earth bond to the core and clamp.`],safety:[`The secondary produces mains voltage whenever the bridge runs, including from the battery alone.`,`Heavy: lift with correct posture or two people.`],related:[`mosfet`,`inductor`,`relay`,`ac-output`,`protective-earth`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Rating`,value:`3`,unit:`kVA`,kind:`model`},{name:`Primary voltage at full modulation`,value:`≈ 30`,unit:`V rms`,kind:`model`},{name:`Secondary voltage`,value:`230`,unit:`V rms`,kind:`model`},{name:`Peak flux density, grain-oriented steel`,value:`≈ 1.2–1.5`,unit:`T`,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Core cross-section follows from V_rms = 4.44·f·N·A_e·B_max, with B_max ≈ 1.2–1.5 T for grain-oriented steel. Any DC offset in the bridge drive walks the core into saturation, so the controller must keep volt-seconds balanced every cycle. The leakage inductance can serve as part of the output filter.`},{id:`inductor`,name:`Power inductor (toroidal choke)`,category:`passive`,symbol:`inductor`,plainSummary:`A coil of copper wire on a ring-shaped core. It resists sudden changes in current, smoothing the chopped PWM waveform into a clean sine wave together with a capacitor.`,function:`Forms the LC output filter with the AC film capacitor, removing switching-frequency ripple so the output is a low-distortion sine wave.`,operatingPrinciple:`Voltage across an inductor is V = L·di/dt: it opposes changes in current by storing energy in its magnetic field (E = ½·L·I²). With a capacitor it forms a low-pass filter whose corner frequency is f_c = 1/(2π√(LC)). Ripple at the PWM frequency, well above f_c, is attenuated at about 40 dB per decade, while the 50 Hz fundamental passes.`,applications:[`Inverter output LC filters`,`Buck/boost converter energy storage (e.g. MPPT charger)`,`EMI common-mode chokes`,`Power-factor correction`],terminals:[{id:`1`,label:`1`,role:`passive`,description:`Winding end 1 (not polarised for a single-winding choke).`},{id:`2`,label:`2`,role:`passive`,description:`Winding end 2.`}],ioBehaviour:`Input: PWM voltage with high-frequency ripple. Output: smoothed current; ripple current ΔI ≈ V·D·(1−D)/(L·f_sw) for a half-bridge leg.`,failureSymptoms:[`Audible whine at the switching frequency (loose winding)`,`Overheating or cracked core (saturation, overload)`,`Distorted output if the core saturates at peak current`,`Open winding: no output`],replacementNotes:[`Match inductance, saturation current and DC resistance.`,`Core material changes the losses; keep the same material (iron powder, ferrite, amorphous).`,`Re-secure with the original clamp; vibration causes noise.`],safety:[`Carries full output current and gets hot.`,`Stored magnetic energy causes an arc if a circuit carrying current is opened.`],related:[`capacitor`,`transformer`,`mosfet`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Output filter inductance`,value:`1.2`,unit:`mH`,kind:`model`},{name:`Rated current`,value:`15`,unit:`A rms`,kind:`model`},{name:`Tolerance of power chokes`,value:`±10–20`,unit:`%`,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Choose L to limit ripple to about 20–40 % of peak current, then choose C for a corner frequency 10–20× below f_sw but well above 50 Hz to avoid resonance with loads. Iron-powder toroids saturate softly (inductance falls gradually), which is forgiving at overload peaks. Ferrite saturates abruptly.`},{id:`capacitor`,name:`Capacitors: electrolytic and film`,category:`passive`,symbol:`capacitor`,plainSummary:`Capacitors store electrical energy for short periods. Large electrolytic "cans" act as reservoirs that steady the DC supply when the switches draw sudden pulses of current. Film capacitors filter the AC output. Big capacitors can stay charged after switch-off, so they are discharged before anyone touches the board.`,function:`Electrolytic bank: reservoir on the DC buses (48 V battery bus, high-voltage PV bus) supplying the switching ripple current. Film capacitor: the C of the output LC filter and snubbers.`,operatingPrinciple:`Charge is stored on two plates separated by a dielectric: Q = C·V, energy E = ½·C·V². Aluminium electrolytics use a very thin oxide layer for large capacitance in a small volume. They are polarised: reverse voltage destroys them. Film capacitors (polypropylene) are non-polarised, have low losses and self-heal small faults, so they suit AC filtering. The equivalent series resistance (ESR) heats the capacitor as ripple current flows: P = I_ripple²·ESR.`,applications:[`DC-link and reservoir banks`,`AC output filters (X2/AC-rated film)`,`Snubbers across switches`,`Decoupling of control ICs`],terminals:[{id:`+`,label:`+ (electrolytic)`,role:`passive`,description:`Positive lead. Snap-in cans are marked by the stripe on the opposite (negative) side; the + lead is unmarked.`},{id:`-`,label:`− (electrolytic)`,role:`passive`,description:`Negative lead, beside the printed stripe of "−" symbols on the sleeve.`},{id:`1`,label:`1 (film)`,role:`passive`,description:`Film capacitor lead; non-polarised.`},{id:`2`,label:`2 (film)`,role:`passive`,description:`Film capacitor lead; non-polarised.`}],ioBehaviour:`Current leads voltage: I = C·dV/dt. On a DC bus the voltage stays almost constant while the capacitor sources and sinks the high-frequency pulse current the switches draw.`,failureSymptoms:[`Bulging or opened vent on the top, electrolyte residue`,`High ESR: increased bus ripple, distorted output, resets under load, overheating`,`Low capacitance: low-voltage shutdowns under sudden load`,`Short circuit: fuse blows at connection, and violent venting is possible`],replacementNotes:[`Match capacitance, voltage rating (never lower), ripple-current rating, ESR and temperature rating (105 °C for power stages).`,`Observe polarity; a reversed electrolytic can explode.`,`Replace all cans in a bank together; they age together.`,`For AC filter positions use AC-rated film capacitors (X2 or "~" rated), never DC types.`],safety:[`Stored energy remains after disconnection. A 940 µF bank at 420 V holds about 83 J, enough to cause a dangerous shock and burns.`,`Never discharge with a screwdriver; use a rated discharge resistor tool and verify with a meter.`,`Wear eye protection near suspect electrolytics; vents can rupture.`],related:[`inductor`,`resistor`,`mosfet`,`voltage-sensor`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`PV-bus electrolytics C5, C6`,value:`470 µF / 450 V`,unit:``,kind:`model`},{name:`Battery-bus electrolytics C1–C4`,value:`4700 µF / 63 V`,unit:``,kind:`model`},{name:`Output film capacitor C7`,value:`10 µF / 275 V~`,unit:``,kind:`model`},{name:`Electrolytic capacitance tolerance`,value:`±20`,unit:`%`,kind:`typical`},{name:`Power-stage electrolytic temperature rating`,value:`105`,unit:`°C`,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Electrolytic life halves for every 10 °C rise (Arrhenius), so rated life at 105 °C is scaled up at the real core temperature, which itself depends on I_ripple²·ESR. Size the bank by ripple current first and capacitance second. In a single-phase inverter the battery bus also carries 100 Hz ripple power, which sets the minimum bulk capacitance.`},{id:`diode`,name:`Rectifier diode`,category:`passive`,symbol:`diode`,plainSummary:`A one-way valve for current. It lets current flow from anode to cathode and blocks it the other way. The silver band marks the cathode.`,function:`Rectifies the auxiliary supply and provides freewheeling and clamp paths; the H-bridge itself uses the MOSFET body diodes.`,operatingPrinciple:`A P-N junction conducts when forward-biased above about 0.6–1.0 V (silicon) and blocks when reverse-biased, up to its rated reverse voltage V_RRM. When switching off, stored charge causes a reverse-recovery current; fast-recovery and Schottky types reduce it.`,applications:[`Bridge rectifiers in chargers`,`Freewheel and clamp paths for inductive loads (relay coils, inductors)`,`Reverse-polarity protection`,`Bootstrap supply for high-side gate drivers`],terminals:[{id:`a`,label:`A (anode)`,role:`passive`,description:`Unmarked end.`},{id:`k`,label:`K (cathode)`,role:`passive`,description:`End with the silver/white band.`}],ioBehaviour:`Forward: conducts with ≈0.9 V drop at rated current (1N5408). Reverse: blocks up to 1000 V with microamp leakage.`,failureSymptoms:[`Shorted: fuse blows or auxiliary supply collapses`,`Open: no rectified output, relay coil spikes damage the driver`,`Cracked or darkened body`],replacementNotes:[`Match V_RRM, average current, and recovery speed (standard, fast, ultrafast, Schottky).`,`Band orientation must match the silkscreen.`],safety:[`Can be at mains or bus potential in operation.`],related:[`mosfet`,`relay`,`resistor`,`capacitor`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`1N5408 repetitive reverse voltage`,value:`1000`,unit:`V`,kind:`part-type`},{name:`1N5408 average forward current`,value:`3`,unit:`A`,kind:`part-type`},{name:`Forward voltage of silicon rectifiers at rated current`,value:`≈ 0.9–1.2`,unit:`V`,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`In hard-switched converters, diode reverse recovery adds turn-on loss to the opposing switch; replace standard diodes with ultrafast or SiC Schottky types in high-frequency positions. Conduction loss is ≈ V_F0·I_avg + r_d·I_rms².`},{id:`resistor`,name:`Resistor (bleeder / power)`,category:`passive`,symbol:`resistor`,plainSummary:`A component that limits current. Colour bands give its value. Across the large capacitors, bleed resistors slowly drain the stored charge after switch-off.`,function:`Bleeder resistors discharge the high-voltage capacitor bank after shutdown; other resistors set gate drive speed, sense current and divide voltages.`,operatingPrinciple:`Ohm's law: V = I·R, with power dissipated P = V²/R = I²·R as heat. With a capacitor C, a bleeder forms an RC circuit whose voltage decays as V(t) = V₀·e^(−t/RC); after one time constant τ = RC, the voltage has fallen to 37 %.`,applications:[`Capacitor bleeders`,`Gate resistors controlling switching speed`,`Current-sense shunts`,`Voltage dividers for feedback`],terminals:[{id:`1`,label:`1`,role:`passive`,description:`Lead 1 (non-polarised).`},{id:`2`,label:`2`,role:`passive`,description:`Lead 2 (non-polarised).`}],ioBehaviour:`Linear: current is proportional to voltage. Two 47 kΩ bleeders in series across a 420 V bank draw 420/94 000 ≈ 4.5 mA and dissipate 1.9 W in total, about 0.94 W each, well inside their 2 W rating. Series connection also halves the voltage across each resistor.`,failureSymptoms:[`Open bleeder: capacitors stay charged for a very long time (shock hazard)`,`Burnt or cracked body, value drifted high`,`Discoloured PCB under overheating resistors`],replacementNotes:[`Match resistance, power rating and voltage rating (high-voltage bleeders need ≥ 500 V element rating, often by using several in series).`,`Keep the same spacing from the PCB for cooling.`],safety:[`An open bleeder means the capacitors stay dangerous; always verify discharge with a meter rather than relying on the bleeder.`],related:[`capacitor`,`voltage-sensor`,`mosfet`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Bleeder value (each)`,value:`47`,unit:`kΩ`,kind:`model`},{name:`Bleeder power rating (each)`,value:`2`,unit:`W`,kind:`model`},{name:`Gold tolerance band`,value:`±5`,unit:`%`,kind:`typical`},{name:`Derating at high ambient temperature`,value:`use ≤ 50–60 % of rated power`,unit:``,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Bleeder sizing is a trade-off: smaller R discharges faster (product standards commonly require the voltage to fall to a safe level within a stated time) but wastes V²/R continuously during operation. Here, 2 × 47 kΩ across 940 µF gives τ ≈ 88 s. Derate film/metal resistors to about 50–60 % of rated power at high ambient temperature.`}],r=[{id:`relay`,name:`Power relay`,category:`protection`,symbol:`relay`,plainSummary:`An electrically operated switch. A small current in its coil pulls metal contacts together, switching the AC output or the grid input on and off. You can hear it click.`,function:`Output and transfer relay: connects the inverter output to the load, and switches between grid pass-through and inverter mode.`,operatingPrinciple:`Current in the coil magnetises an iron core, attracting an armature that moves the contacts: COM connects to NO (normally open) when energised and to NC (normally closed) when released. A diode across the coil absorbs the inductive voltage spike when the drive transistor turns off. Contacts are rated separately for AC and DC; DC breaking capacity is much lower because the arc has no zero crossing.`,applications:[`Output and grid-transfer switching`,`Pre-charge bypass of inrush resistors`,`Fan and auxiliary control`],terminals:[{id:`a1`,label:`A1 (coil +)`,role:`control`,description:`Coil supply (12 V DC).`},{id:`a2`,label:`A2 (coil −)`,role:`control`,description:`Coil return, switched by a transistor.`},{id:`com`,label:`COM`,role:`power-in`,description:`Common contact.`},{id:`no`,label:`NO`,role:`power-out`,description:`Normally open: connected to COM when the coil is energised.`},{id:`nc`,label:`NC`,role:`power-out`,description:`Normally closed: connected to COM when the coil is off.`}],ioBehaviour:`Input: 12 V coil voltage (~ 0.9 W). Output: contacts rated 30 A at 250 V AC. Operate time ≈ 10 ms.`,failureSymptoms:[`No click, no output (coil open, driver failed)`,`Clicks but no output (burnt contacts)`,`Welded contacts: output stays on, cannot transfer`,`Chattering under load (low coil voltage)`],replacementNotes:[`Match coil voltage, contact form and AC/DC contact ratings.`,`Check the coil suppression diode.`],safety:[`Contacts carry mains voltage.`,`An NC contact can connect grid to output when the inverter is off.`],related:[`diode`,`microcontroller`,`ac-output`,`fuse`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Coil voltage`,value:`12`,unit:`V DC`,kind:`model`},{name:`Contact rating`,value:`30 A / 250 V~`,unit:``,kind:`model`},{name:`Coil resistance for a 12 V, 0.9 W coil (R = V²/P)`,value:`≈ 160`,unit:`Ω`,kind:`model`},{name:`Operate time of power relays`,value:`≈ 5–15`,unit:`ms`,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Close relays near the voltage zero crossing and open them near the current zero crossing to minimise arcing and contact erosion. For grid-transfer, interlocks must prevent the inverter output ever being connected to a live grid without synchronisation.`},{id:`fuse`,name:`Battery fuse (bolt-down MEGA type)`,category:`protection`,symbol:`fuse`,plainSummary:`A deliberate weak link. If a short circuit makes the current far too high, the metal strip inside melts and breaks the circuit before cables catch fire.`,function:`Protects the battery cables and inverter against short-circuit currents from the battery, which can reach thousands of amps.`,operatingPrinciple:`The fuse element heats with I²·R; above its rating it melts after a time that falls steeply with current (the time–current curve). The arc is quenched inside the body. The DC voltage rating and breaking capacity must exceed the system voltage and the battery's prospective short-circuit current.`,applications:[`Battery positive conductors`,`Inverter DC inputs`,`PV string protection (gPV fuses)`],terminals:[{id:`1`,label:`Bolt 1`,role:`power-in`,description:`Battery side; non-polarised.`},{id:`2`,label:`Bolt 2`,role:`power-out`,description:`Inverter side.`}],ioBehaviour:`Conducts with a tiny voltage drop (tens of mV) up to rated current; opens permanently on overcurrent.`,failureSymptoms:[`Inverter completely dead, battery voltage not detected`,`Visible break or blackening through the window`,`Warm fuse in normal use: loose bolts or undersized fuse`],replacementNotes:[`Same type, current rating, DC voltage rating and breaking capacity, never higher.`,`Torque bolts correctly; a loose bolt overheats.`],safety:[`Batteries deliver enormous fault current; remove rings and watches and use insulated tools.`,`Never bypass a fuse.`],related:[`battery-terminals`,`circuit-breaker`,`mosfet`,`capacitor`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Rated current`,value:`150`,unit:`A`,kind:`model`},{name:`DC voltage rating needed for a 48 V system`,value:`≥ 60`,unit:`V`,kind:`typical`},{name:`Breaking capacity`,value:`must exceed the battery’s prospective short-circuit current`,unit:``,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Rate the fuse at about 125 % of maximum continuous battery current (3 kW / (0.9 × 46 V) ≈ 72 A → 100–150 A with surge), but below the cable's ampacity, so the cable is always protected. Connecting the inverter's discharged bus capacitors causes an inrush that can nuisance-blow small fuses; a pre-charge circuit prevents this.`},{id:`circuit-breaker`,name:`DC circuit breaker (2-pole, polarised)`,category:`protection`,symbol:`circuit-breaker`,plainSummary:`A switch that turns itself off when there is too much current, and can be reset. It also lets you safely disconnect the solar panels or battery for maintenance.`,function:`PV input isolator and overcurrent protection; provides a visible, lockable disconnection point for safe maintenance.`,operatingPrinciple:`A bimetal strip bends with sustained overload heating (thermal trip), and a solenoid trips instantly on short-circuit current (magnetic trip). Contacts open inside an arc chute that stretches and cools the arc. Polarised DC breakers use a permanent magnet to drive the arc into the chute, so they must be wired with the marked polarity and direction.`,applications:[`PV array isolation`,`Battery disconnects`,`DC distribution`],terminals:[{id:`in+`,label:`+ line (top)`,role:`power-in`,description:`Positive from the PV array, marked "+" and an arrow.`},{id:`out+`,label:`+ load (bottom)`,role:`power-out`,description:`Positive to the charger.`},{id:`in-`,label:`− line (top)`,role:`power-in`,description:`Negative from the PV array.`},{id:`out-`,label:`− load (bottom)`,role:`power-out`,description:`Negative to the charger.`}],ioBehaviour:`Closed: negligible drop. Trips on overload per its curve (C curve: magnetic trip at 5–10 × I_n).`,failureSymptoms:[`Trips repeatedly (real overload, earth fault, or worn mechanism)`,`Will not reset (fault still present)`,`Hot terminals (loose connection)`],replacementNotes:[`Must be DC-rated at or above the maximum PV open-circuit voltage at the coldest temperature.`,`Observe polarity marking; reversed wiring of a polarised breaker can fail to interrupt.`],safety:[`The line side remains live from the PV array in daylight even when the breaker is off.`,`Lock off and tag before work.`],related:[`solar-input`,`fuse`,`igbt`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Rated current`,value:`20`,unit:`A`,kind:`model`},{name:`Rated DC voltage needed`,value:`≥ 450 (max PV V_oc)`,unit:`V`,kind:`model`},{name:`Poles`,value:`2`,unit:``,kind:`model`},{name:`C-curve magnetic trip`,value:`5–10 × I_n`,unit:``,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`PV open-circuit voltage rises as cells get colder (≈ −0.3 %/°C coefficient), so size the breaker and inverter input for V_oc at the minimum site temperature. Two-pole switching isolates both conductors, as required for floating (unearthed) arrays.`},{id:`voltage-sensor`,name:`Isolated voltage sensing module`,category:`measurement`,symbol:`voltage-sensor`,plainSummary:`A small circuit that measures a high voltage safely. It scales it down to a tiny signal the microcontroller can read, and keeps the dangerous voltage separated from the controller.`,function:`Measures the AC output and DC bus voltages for regulation, protection (over/under-voltage) and display.`,operatingPrinciple:`A high-value resistor divider scales the voltage down (e.g. 1:200). An isolation amplifier transfers the scaled signal across an insulation barrier (capacitive or optical) and outputs a 0–3.3 V signal, offset to mid-scale for AC, to the MCU's ADC.`,applications:[`Inverter output regulation`,`Battery and PV voltage measurement`,`Grid synchronisation`],terminals:[{id:`in+`,label:`IN+ (HV)`,role:`sense`,description:`High-voltage input, through the divider chain.`},{id:`in-`,label:`IN− (HV)`,role:`sense`,description:`High-voltage reference.`},{id:`vcc`,label:`VCC`,role:`power-in`,description:`3.3 V/5 V low-voltage side supply.`},{id:`gnd`,label:`GND`,role:`ground`,description:`Low-voltage side ground.`},{id:`out`,label:`OUT`,role:`sense`,description:`Scaled analogue output to the ADC.`}],ioBehaviour:`Input: e.g. 0–400 V. Output: 0–3.3 V analogue, linear (within ±1 %).`,failureSymptoms:[`Output voltage regulated wrongly (too high or too low)`,`False over/under-voltage faults`,`Display shows 0 V while output is present`],replacementNotes:[`Use the same divider ratio and isolation rating; recalibrate in firmware if available.`],safety:[`Input side is at hazardous voltage; isolation barrier must not be bridged by probes.`],related:[`microcontroller`,`resistor`,`current-sensor`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Input range`,value:`0–400`,unit:`V`,kind:`model`},{name:`Output range to the ADC`,value:`0–3.3`,unit:`V`,kind:`model`},{name:`Isolation rating`,value:`several kV rms for reinforced isolation`,unit:``,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`A drifting feedback sensor is a classic cause of "output voltage too high": the controller faithfully regulates to a wrong reading. Divider resistors at high voltage must be rated for voltage as well as power, hence several in series.`},{id:`current-sensor`,name:`Hall-effect current sensor`,category:`measurement`,symbol:`current-sensor`,plainSummary:`Measures how much current flows through a busbar passing through its hole, without touching it electrically. The controller uses this to protect against overloads and short circuits.`,function:`Measures battery/bridge current for overload and short-circuit protection, current control and power calculation.`,operatingPrinciple:`The conductor passes through a ferrite core with an air gap. A Hall element in the gap produces a voltage proportional to the magnetic flux, and so to current. The sensor outputs V_out = V_ref + I × sensitivity, with V_ref at mid-supply (2.5 V) so both current directions can be measured. It is galvanically isolated by construction.`,applications:[`Inverter overcurrent protection`,`Battery charge/discharge measurement`,`Motor current control`],terminals:[{id:`v5`,label:`+5 V`,role:`power-in`,description:`Sensor supply.`},{id:`gnd`,label:`0 V`,role:`ground`,description:`Ground.`},{id:`out`,label:`OUT`,role:`sense`,description:`Analogue output, 2.5 V at zero current.`},{id:`ref`,label:`REF`,role:`sense`,description:`Reference voltage output (mid-scale).`}],ioBehaviour:`Input: primary current through the aperture (±100 A nominal here). Output: 2.5 V ± 0.625 V at nominal current (6.25 mV/A, illustrative). Pin order varies by manufacturer; always confirm with the datasheet.`,failureSymptoms:[`Overload fault at light load (offset drift)`,`No protection on real overload (sensor dead)`,`Power reading wrong on display`],replacementNotes:[`Match range, sensitivity and supply voltage; aperture size must fit the busbar.`],safety:[`The busbar through the sensor carries high current and may be at battery or bus potential.`],related:[`microcontroller`,`voltage-sensor`,`mosfet`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Nominal measuring range`,value:`±100`,unit:`A`,kind:`model`},{name:`Zero-current output (mid-supply)`,value:`2.5`,unit:`V`,kind:`model`},{name:`Sensitivity used in this model`,value:`6.25`,unit:`mV/A (illustrative)`,kind:`model`}],advancedNote:`Open-loop Hall sensors have offset and gain drift with temperature; closed-loop (compensated) sensors are more accurate and faster. For short-circuit protection, the sensor bandwidth (≥ 100 kHz) and a hardware comparator matter more than accuracy.`},{id:`heat-sink`,name:`Heat sink (extruded aluminium)`,category:`cooling`,symbol:`heat-sink`,plainSummary:`A finned block of aluminium that pulls heat out of the power switches and spreads it into the air. Without it the switches would overheat in seconds.`,function:`Removes the conduction and switching losses from the MOSFETs and IGBTs and keeps their junction temperature within limits.`,operatingPrinciple:`Heat flows from the silicon junction through the device case, the insulating thermal pad, and into the aluminium. Fins increase the surface area for convection into the airflow from the fan. The temperature rise follows a thermal "Ohm's law": T_j = T_ambient + P_loss·(R_θjc + R_θcs + R_θsa), in °C/W.`,applications:[`Power semiconductors in all converters`,`Rectifier bridges`,`Linear regulators`],terminals:[{id:`earth`,label:`⏚ Earth bond`,role:`earth`,description:`Bonding screw to protective earth; devices are insulated from it.`}],ioBehaviour:`Thermal: with forced air, R_θsa ≈ 0.3 °C/W; without the fan it rises to about 1.2 °C/W.`,failureSymptoms:[`Overheating shutdown (blocked fins, dust, fan failure)`,`Device failure after a thermal pad is missing or torn`,`Insulation breakdown between tab and heat sink (shorts to earth)`],replacementNotes:[`Clean old thermal compound; fit new pads, bushes and correct screw torque.`,`Ensure fins align with the fan airflow.`],safety:[`Can exceed 80 °C: burns.`,`If an insulator fails the heat sink becomes live; this is why it is earthed.`],related:[`mosfet`,`igbt`,`cooling-fan`,`protective-earth`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`R_θsa with forced air`,value:`0.3`,unit:`°C/W`,kind:`model`},{name:`R_θsa without the fan`,value:`1.2`,unit:`°C/W`,kind:`model`},{name:`Common extrusion alloy`,value:`6063 aluminium`,unit:``,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Example: 60 W total loss at 40 °C ambient with R_θsa = 0.3 °C/W gives a heat-sink temperature of 58 °C. Each device adds its own R_θjc + R_θcs (≈ 0.6 °C/W per device including the pad), so junctions sit about 10 °C hotter again. Losing the fan quadruples R_θsa, giving a heat-sink temperature over 110 °C, which is why fan failure leads to thermal shutdown.`},{id:`cooling-fan`,name:`Cooling fan (12 V brushless)`,category:`cooling`,symbol:`cooling-fan`,plainSummary:`Blows air across the heat sink so the switches stay cool. The controller speeds it up when the inverter works hard.`,function:`Forces air through the heat-sink fins and enclosure; speed is controlled by temperature.`,operatingPrinciple:`A brushless DC motor with an electronically commutated stator drives the impeller. The tachometer output gives two pulses per revolution so the controller can detect a stalled fan. Speed is varied by PWM or supply voltage.`,applications:[`Inverters, chargers, power supplies, computers`],terminals:[{id:`v12`,label:`+12 V (red)`,role:`power-in`,description:`Supply.`},{id:`gnd`,label:`GND (black)`,role:`ground`,description:`Return.`},{id:`tach`,label:`TACH (yellow)`,role:`sense`,description:`Speed signal, 2 pulses per revolution (open collector).`}],ioBehaviour:`Input: 12 V, about 0.3 A. Output: airflow (about 30 CFM) and tach pulses at 2 × rpm / 60 Hz.`,failureSymptoms:[`Fan does not spin: over-temperature faults under load`,`Noisy bearing, slow speed`,`"Fan fault" code from missing tach pulses`],replacementNotes:[`Match size, voltage, airflow, and connector/tach type.`,`Fit with airflow direction arrow pointing into the heat-sink fins.`],safety:[`Keep fingers clear; disconnect before cleaning.`],related:[`heat-sink`,`microcontroller`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Size`,value:`80`,unit:`mm`,kind:`model`},{name:`Supply`,value:`12`,unit:`V DC`,kind:`model`},{name:`Current`,value:`0.3`,unit:`A`,kind:`model`},{name:`Tachometer output`,value:`2 pulses per revolution`,unit:``,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Fan bearings are a leading wear-out item in inverters; ball-bearing fans outlast sleeve bearings at high temperature. Fan laws: airflow ∝ rpm, pressure ∝ rpm², power ∝ rpm³, so modest speed reduction greatly cuts noise and power.`}],i=[{id:`enclosure`,name:`Enclosure (chassis and cover)`,category:`io-connection`,symbol:`protective-earth`,plainSummary:`The metal case. It protects people from live parts inside, protects the electronics from dust and knocks, guides cooling air, and is connected to earth for safety.`,function:`Provides mechanical support, protection against contact with live parts, airflow paths for cooling, electromagnetic shielding and a bonded protective-earth frame.`,operatingPrinciple:`Powder-coated sheet steel forms an earthed Faraday enclosure: radiated switching noise is contained and any internal insulation fault to the case is cleared by the protective devices. Vent slots and the fan create a front-to-back or side-to-side airflow path; the IP rating states how well it excludes dust and water.`,applications:[`All mains-powered equipment`,`Wall-mounted inverters (typically IP20 indoors, IP65 outdoors)`],terminals:[{id:`pe-bond`,label:`PE bond`,role:`earth`,description:`Bonded to the PE stud; every panel must keep its earth connection.`}],ioBehaviour:`Passive; carries fault current to earth only during an insulation failure.`,failureSymptoms:[`Overheating from blocked vents or dust`,`Corrosion at earth contact points`,`Rattles or hum from loose panels`],replacementNotes:[`Reinstall every screw and earth strap; star washers must bite bare metal.`],safety:[`Never operate with the cover removed except during qualified testing.`,`Sharp sheet-metal edges.`],related:[`protective-earth`,`cooling-fan`,`heat-sink`],typicalSpecs:[{name:`Sheet steel thickness`,value:`≈ 1–1.5`,unit:`mm`,kind:`typical`},{name:`Indoor protection rating`,value:`IP20`,unit:``,kind:`typical`}],advancedNote:`Cover removal is the first step after isolation because the nameplate warning ("wait 5 min after disconnection") refers to capacitor discharge inside. Panel seams and vent sizes are chosen against EMC as well as cooling requirements.`}],a=[...n,...e,...r,...t],o=Object.fromEntries(a.map(e=>[e.id,e])),s={...o,...Object.fromEntries(i.map(e=>[e.id,e]))};export{o as n,s as r,a as t};